ear/14053808/state.json" "file_sha256" "5f5c55c1dee1ff993ca21ac73c69c34ef9fcc84fd77e4a3a4dae51e796de9e62" "basis" 1789 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14053808/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-10076" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "x=42,y=-35,z=9"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14053808/state.json" "file_sha256" "5f5c55c1dee1ff993ca21ac73c69c34ef9fcc84fd77e4a3a4dae51e796de9e62" "basis" 1789 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14053808/edb-import/verification.json"}] "sources" [{"question" "q-10076" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Solve the following system of linear equations by finding the inverse of the coefficient matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}4x-2y+3z=0 \\\\ 8x-3y+5z=9 \\\\ 7x-2y+4z=-1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061091/state.json" "file_sha256" "07cb7941685ba925abcd3fba5cedc076a5d94d819ac934cab441c748dc51337f"} {"question" "q-10076" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We wish to write this system in the form $Ax=b$, where $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and $x$ and $b$ are $3\\times 1$ column vectors. We can do this as follows:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 8 & -3 & 5 \\\\ 7 & -2 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 9 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, the solution to $Ax=b$ is given by $x={A}^{-1}b.$ So, we compute ${A}^{-1}$ as follows:\n\n*Step 1:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =|\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 8 & -3 & 5 \\\\ 7 & -2 & 4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =4|\\begin{aligned}-3 & 5 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}|-(-2)|\\begin{aligned}8 & 5 \\\\ 7 & 4\\end{aligned}|+3|\\begin{aligned}8 & -3 \\\\ 7 & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =4(-12+10)+2(32-35)+3(-16+21) \\\\  & =-8-6+15 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Step 2:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}-3 & 5 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}8 & 5 \\\\ 7 & 4\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}8 & -3 \\\\ 7 & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ 7 & 4\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 \\\\ 7 & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 \\\\ -3 & 5\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 \\\\ 8 & 5\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}4 & -2 \\\\ 8 & -3\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & -3 & 5 \\\\ -2 & -5 & 6 \\\\ -1 & -4 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Step 3:*\n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 3 & 5 \\\\ 2 & -5 & -6 \\\\ -1 & 4 & 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n*Step 4:*\n\n$$\n{C}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -5 & 4 \\\\ 5 & -6 & 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n*Step 5:*\n\n$$\n{A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T}=\\frac{1}{1}[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -5 & 4 \\\\ 5 & -6 & 4\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nHence\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 2 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -5 & 4 \\\\ 5 & -6 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 9 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}19 \\\\ -49 \\\\ -58\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence the solution is $x=19,y=-49,z=-58.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061091/state.json" "file_sha256" "07cb7941685ba925abcd3fba5cedc076a5d94d819ac934cab441c748dc51337f"} {"question" "q-10076" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061091/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "879804538ba3697e287382508287962df7f6480d94d3e8e9ee24e943227ad6f8"} {"question" "q-10076" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061091/state.json" "file_sha256" "07cb7941685ba925abcd3fba5cedc076a5d94d819ac934cab441c748dc51337f"} {"question" "q-10076" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x=-19,y=-49,z=-58"] ["math" "x=15,y=-49,z=-58"] ["math" "x=19,y=-49,z=-58"] ["math" "x=19,y=49,z=58"] ["math" "x=42,y=-35,z=9"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061091/state.json" "file_sha256" "07cb7941685ba925abcd3fba5cedc076a5d94d819ac934cab441c748dc51337f"} {"question" "q-10076" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x=19,y=-49,z=-58"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061091/state.json" "file_sha256" "07cb7941685ba925abcd3fba5cedc076a5d94d819ac934cab441c748dc51337f"} {"question" "q-10102" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Solve the following system of linear equations by finding the inverse of the coefficient matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}2y+2z=1 \\\\ x+y+z=3 \\\\ y+2z=-3\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061091/state.json" "file_sha256" "07cb7941685ba925abcd3fba5cedc076a5d94d819ac934cab441c748dc51337f"} {"question" "q-10102" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We wish to write this system in the form $Ax=b$, where $A$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix and $x$ and $b$ are $3\\times 1$ column vectors. We can do this as follows:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, the solution to $Ax=b$ is given by $x={A}^{-1}b.$ So, we compute ${A}^{-1}$ as follows:\n\n*Step 1:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}det(A) & =|\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =0|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}|-2|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}|+2|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =0-2(2)+2(1) \\\\  & =-4+2 \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Step 2:*\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}| & |\\begin{aligned}0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -2 & -2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n*Step 3:*\n\n$$\nC=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n*Step 4:*\n\n$$\n{C}^{T}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n*Step 5:*\n\n$$\n{A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{det(A)}{C}^{T}=-\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]=\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nHence\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -3\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 8 \\\\ -7\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ 4 \\\\ -\\frac{7}{2}\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence the solution is $x=\\frac{5}{2},y=4,z=-\\frac{7}{2}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061091/state.json" "file_sha256" "07cb7941685ba925abcd3fba5cedc076a5d94d819ac934cab441c748dc51337f"} {"question" "q-10102" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061091/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "879804538ba3697e287382508287962df7f6480d94d3e8e9ee24e943227ad6f8"} {"question" "q-10102" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061091/state.json" "file_sha256" "07cb7941685ba925abcd3fba5cedc076a5d94d819ac934cab441c748dc51337f"} {"question" "q-10102" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x=-5,y=8,z=-7"] ["math" "x=-\\frac{5}{2},y=4,z=-\\frac{7}{2}"] ["math" "x=5,y=8,z=-7"] ["math" "x=\\frac{5}{2},y=-4,z=-\\frac{7}{2}"] ["math" "x=\\frac{5}{2},y=4,z=-\\frac{7}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061091/state.json" "file_sha256" "07cb7941685ba925abcd3fba5cedc076a5d94d819ac934cab441c748dc51337f"} {"question" "q-10102" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x=\\frac{5}{2},y=4,z=-\\frac{7}{2}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061091/state.json" "file_sha256" "07cb7941685ba925abcd3fba5cedc076a5d94d819ac934cab441c748dc51337f"} {"question" "q-78445" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider a matrix equation $AB\\,x=d,$ where $A$ and $B$ are $4\\times 4$ non-singular matrices, while $x$ and $d$ are $4\\times 1$ column vectors. Solve the equation for $x.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061091/state.json" "file_sha256" "07cb7941685ba925abcd3fba5cedc076a5d94d819ac934cab441c748dc51337f"} {"question" "q-78445" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $A$ is not singular, it must have an inverse ${A}^{-1}.$ Now, pre-multiplying both sides of the equation $AB\\,x=d$ by ${A}^{-1},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}AB\\,x & =d \\\\ {A}^{-1}AB\\,x & ={A}^{-1}\\,d \\\\ {I}_{4}B\\,x & ={A}^{-1}\\,d \\\\ B\\,x & ={A}^{-1}\\,d.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $B$ is not singular, it must have an inverse ${B}^{-1}.$ Now, pre-multiplying both sides of the equation $B\\,x={A}^{-1}\\,d$ by ${B}^{-1},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B\\,x & ={A}^{-1}\\,d \\\\ {B}^{-1}B\\,x & ={B}^{-1}{A}^{-1}\\,d \\\\ {I}_{4}\\,x & ={B}^{-1}{A}^{-1}\\,d \\\\ x & ={B}^{-1}{A}^{-1}\\,d.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061091/state.json" "file_sha256" "07cb7941685ba925abcd3fba5cedc076a5d94d819ac934cab441c748dc51337f"} {"question" "q-78445" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061091/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "879804538ba3697e287382508287962df7f6480d94d3e8e9ee24e943227ad6f8"} {"question" "q-78445" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061091/state.json" "file_sha256" "07cb7941685ba925abcd3fba5cedc076a5d94d819ac934cab441c748dc51337f"} {"question" "q-78445" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x=BA\\,d"] ["math" "x={A}^{-1}\\,d\\,B"] ["math" "x={A}^{-1}{B}^{-1}\\,d"] ["math" "x={B}^{-1}\\,d\\,{A}^{-1}"] ["math" "x={B}^{-1}{A}^{-1}\\,d"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061091/state.json" "file_sha256" "07cb7941685ba925abcd3fba5cedc076a5d94d819ac934cab441c748dc51337f"} {"question" "q-78445" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x={B}^{-1}{A}^{-1}\\,d"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061091/state.json" "file_sha256" "07cb7941685ba925abcd3fba5cedc076a5d94d819ac934cab441c748dc51337f"} {"question" "q-78555" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-2x-y-z=3 \\\\ 6x-3y-5z=5 \\\\ 5x-y-2z=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system above has the matrix representation $Ax=b.$ Given that $x=⟨x,y,z⟩$ is unique, what is the value of $x+y+z?$ \n\n*Hint: The inverse of the coefficient matrix is given by*\n\n$$\n\\,{A}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ -13 & 9 & -16 \\\\ 9 & -7 & 12\\end{aligned}].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061091/state.json" "file_sha256" "07cb7941685ba925abcd3fba5cedc076a5d94d819ac934cab441c748dc51337f"} {"question" "q-78555" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's write our system in matrix form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Ax & =b \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-2 & -1 & -1 \\\\ 6 & -3 & -5 \\\\ 5 & -1 & -2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that the solution to $Ax=b$ is given by $x={A}^{-1}b.$\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={A}^{-1}b \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ -13 & 9 & -16 \\\\ 9 & -7 & 12\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solution is $x=0,y=-5,z=2.$\n\nFinally, $x+y+z=0+(-5)+2=-3.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061091/state.json" "file_sha256" "07cb7941685ba925abcd3fba5cedc076a5d94d819ac934cab441c748dc51337f"} {"question" "q-78555" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061091/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "879804538ba3697e287382508287962df7f6480d94d3e8e9ee24e943227ad6f8"} {"question" "q-78555" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061091/state.json" "file_sha256" "07cb7941685ba925abcd3fba5cedc076a5d94d819ac934cab441c748dc51337f"} {"question" "q-78555" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-2"] ["math" "-3"] ["math" "-7"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "5"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061091/state.json" "file_sha256" "07cb7941685ba925abcd3fba5cedc076a5d94d819ac934cab441c748dc51337f"} {"question" "q-78555" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-3"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061091/state.json" "file_sha256" "07cb7941685ba925abcd3fba5cedc076a5d94d819ac934cab441c748dc51337f"} {"question" "q-317900" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider a system of $3$ linear equations in $3$ variables, written in matrix form as $A\\,x=b.$ Here, $A$ is the non-singular coefficient matrix of the system, $x$ is the column vector containing the variables, and $b$ is the column vector containing the constants. What are the missing parts of the derivation method shown below to find the unique solution of the system?\n\n$$\nA\\,x={{field-1}}\n$$\n\n$$\n{{field-2}}\\,x={A}^{-1}\\,b\n$$\n\n$$\n{I}_{3}\\,x={A}^{-1}\\,{{field-3}}\n$$\n\n$$\n{{field-4}}={A}^{-1}\\,b\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061091/state.json" "file_sha256" "07cb7941685ba925abcd3fba5cedc076a5d94d819ac934cab441c748dc51337f"} {"question" "q-317900" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "Since $A$ is not singular, it must have an inverse ${A}^{-1}.$ Now, pre-multiplying both sides of the equation $A\\,x=b$ by ${A}^{-1},$ we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A\\,x & =b \\\\ {A}^{-1}A\\,x & ={A}^{-1}\\,b \\\\ {I}_{3}\\,x & ={A}^{-1}\\,b \\\\ x & ={A}^{-1}\\,b\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061091/state.json" "file_sha256" "07cb7941685ba925abcd3fba5cedc076a5d94d819ac934cab441c748dc51337f"} {"question" "q-317900" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061091/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "879804538ba3697e287382508287962df7f6480d94d3e8e9ee24e943227ad6f8"} {"question" "q-317900" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061091/state.json" "file_sha256" "07cb7941685ba925abcd3fba5cedc076a5d94d819ac934cab441c748dc51337f"} {"question" "q-317900" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "A"] ["math" "b"] ["math" "x"] ["math" "{A}^{-1}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14061091/state.json" "file_sha256" "07cb7941685ba925abcd3fba5cedc076a5d94d819ac934cab44